WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:07.689
let's go to the phones at (617) 830-4750 and
see what's on people's minds.

00:00:07.689 --> 00:00:11.800
Starting with our caller from the four zero
one area code.

00:00:11.800 --> 00:00:13.780
Who's calling today from four zero one

00:00:13.780 --> 00:00:16.730
Hey David, my name's Kenny.

00:00:16.730 --> 00:00:18.240
How you doing?

00:00:18.240 --> 00:00:19.760
How's it going?

00:00:19.760 --> 00:00:20.760
I'm doing well.

00:00:20.760 --> 00:00:25.060
Um, first time caller, long time listener,
uh, recently picked up a membership since

00:00:25.060 --> 00:00:28.369
you've been doing such great coverage of the
primaries.

00:00:28.369 --> 00:00:29.410
I wanted to call.

00:00:29.410 --> 00:00:33.460
So I'm actually out here at UC Berkeley on
a graduate student and I'm sure you've heard

00:00:33.460 --> 00:00:38.050
about the strike that's going on in Santa
Cruz.

00:00:38.050 --> 00:00:41.870
I wanted to get your take on it because I'm
a pretty active member with the union here

00:00:41.870 --> 00:00:46.610
on campus and as you know, this is a Wildcat
strike that, uh, isn't endorsed by the union

00:00:46.610 --> 00:00:50.920
officially and, uh, for political reasons
I won't be putting in my 2 cents, but, uh,

00:00:50.920 --> 00:00:52.859
I want to get your take next.

00:00:52.859 --> 00:00:56.879
You know, I just don't really know enough
about the circumstances to be able to comment.

00:00:56.879 --> 00:01:01.340
I know that when I was an undergrad at the
university of Massachusetts, there would very

00:01:01.340 --> 00:01:08.340
often be a varieties of different types of
either protests or strikes from the graduate

00:01:08.340 --> 00:01:11.680
student, uh, organizations or different groups.

00:01:11.680 --> 00:01:15.580
And I, I just feel like it would be inappropriate
for me to really weigh in because I don't,

00:01:15.580 --> 00:01:16.580
I'm aware of it.

00:01:16.580 --> 00:01:21.730
I don't know all the circumstances and, uh,
I just don't think there's any, any benefit

00:01:21.730 --> 00:01:24.710
to me giving an uninformed opinion about something
so specific.

00:01:24.710 --> 00:01:29.200
So then can I ask you a more general question?

00:01:29.200 --> 00:01:34.850
Um, given what we did, what we've seen recently
with big labor pushing back on things like

00:01:34.850 --> 00:01:40.560
Medicare for all and attacking the Sanders
agenda because of some of the quote unquote

00:01:40.560 --> 00:01:42.750
things that they fought for so far.

00:01:42.750 --> 00:01:46.840
Um, what role do you think big labor has to
play in something like this?

00:01:46.840 --> 00:01:50.880
Do you think that worker strikes can be effective
without big labor?

00:01:50.880 --> 00:01:56.140
Um, and where do you think that or what do
you think the university of California, I

00:01:56.140 --> 00:01:57.140
guess that's specific.

00:01:57.140 --> 00:01:58.140
So let's, let's just stop there.

00:01:58.140 --> 00:01:59.660
As far as big labor goes.

00:01:59.660 --> 00:02:07.340
So I, I believe that it is, um, completely
incorrect to suggest that Medicare for all,

00:02:07.340 --> 00:02:14.700
as an example, is something that big labor
as you call it, should oppose because it takes

00:02:14.700 --> 00:02:21.160
out of, uh, the sort of negotiating package
or what's under the umbrella of big labor

00:02:21.160 --> 00:02:23.070
to do for their union members.

00:02:23.070 --> 00:02:27.490
In fact, I saw an analysis that was, that
was different, which is if you take something

00:02:27.490 --> 00:02:32.430
like healthcare out of the negotiation, big
labor, because you have a Medicare for all

00:02:32.430 --> 00:02:39.980
program, big labor now can specifically know
the number of dollars per employee that were

00:02:39.980 --> 00:02:45.630
previously being spent on health insurance
and can argue that there should be raises

00:02:45.630 --> 00:02:48.400
at least for some part of what that savings
is.

00:02:48.400 --> 00:02:55.620
So I reject completely the idea that a on
principle big labor should be opposed to something

00:02:55.620 --> 00:02:56.630
like Medicare for all.

00:02:56.630 --> 00:03:01.390
And I don't believe all big labor is, I mean,
we heard from some union unions that, uh,

00:03:01.390 --> 00:03:03.730
activate, excuse me, activated around that
issue.

00:03:03.730 --> 00:03:06.290
Um, but it has not been all of them.

00:03:06.290 --> 00:03:08.190
And I, I just don't buy that for a second.

00:03:08.190 --> 00:03:11.760
I think that that's a talking point meant
to stifle supportive, progressive candidates

00:03:11.760 --> 00:03:16.290
and it's a talking point meant to support
a stifle supportive Medicare for all.

00:03:16.290 --> 00:03:17.350
Interesting.

00:03:17.350 --> 00:03:18.400
Yeah.

00:03:18.400 --> 00:03:22.780
David Dolan had a similar, not completely
the same take, but uh, in a similar vein.

00:03:22.780 --> 00:03:23.780
So thanks.

00:03:23.780 --> 00:03:24.780
Um, last thing.

00:03:24.780 --> 00:03:27.880
Where can I find the show being live streamed
right now?

00:03:27.880 --> 00:03:28.880
If I'm a member.

00:03:28.880 --> 00:03:29.880
Yeah.

00:03:29.880 --> 00:03:30.940
Oh we don't, uh, we don't stream the show
live.

00:03:30.940 --> 00:03:34.180
The show is produced in the afternoon and
then released a few hours later.

00:03:34.180 --> 00:03:38.570
So only only like the evening livestreams
are actually available, available live.

00:03:38.570 --> 00:03:39.920
Kenny, thank you so much for the call.

00:03:39.920 --> 00:03:42.220
I appreciate hearing from you.

